.TH CLOG_SET_FORMAT 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
clog_set_format \- change the output format at runtime
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/clogger.h>

void clog_set_format(clog logger, clog_format_t fmt);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Updates the output format of the shared backing store.  The shared
store holds the format, and not the struct of each logger.  The change
is therefore at once visible to every logger handle that writes to the
same file descriptor.  That covers the root logger and every derived
logger.

There are three formats.

  CLOG_FMT_LOGFMT (0)
    The default.  The library writes every record as one logfmt line:
      ts=<ISO-8601-UTC> level=<L> proc=<name>(<pid>):<tname>(<tid>)
      src=<file>:<line> func=<fn> [fields] msg=<text>
    ccol_log_error, ccol_log_alert and ccol_log_fatal append backtrace
    continuation lines after the main record.  A tab indents each one.
    In proc, <tname> and <tid> are the name and the ID of the thread
    that wrote the record.  Each thread reads them once, the first
    time that it logs.  ccol_set_thread_name(3) renames a thread and
    updates that name.

  CLOG_FMT_JSON (1)
    The library writes every record as one NDJSON object, and then a
    newline:
      {"ts":"...","level":"...","proc":"name(pid):tname(tid)",
       "src":"file:N","func":"...", [fields,] "msg":"..."}
    A structured field becomes a top-level JSON key.  For
    ccol_log_error, ccol_log_alert and ccol_log_fatal, the library
    embeds the backtrace as a "bt" array of strings inside the same
    JSON object.  It checks every string value as UTF-8 before it
    embeds that value.  Those values are the message, and the key and
    the value of every field.  It replaces a byte or a sequence of
    bytes that is not well-formed UTF-8, one invalid byte at a time,
    with the Unicode replacement character (U+FFFD).  A message or a
    field value built from arbitrary or binary data can therefore
    never make a record with invalid Unicode content.

  CLOG_FMT_SYSLOG (2)
    The RFC 5424 syslog format.  Only a logger that sits on an fd
    offers it, which is one from clog_open_fd or clog_open_fd_mp.  A
    call to clog_set_format() with CLOG_FMT_SYSLOG on a logger that
    sits on a file does nothing, and it reports nothing.  The format
    stays as it was.

    The library writes each record as one line:
      <PRI>1 TIMESTAMP HOSTNAME APP-NAME PID MSGID [ccol ...] MSG\en

      PRI        = (facility * 8) + RFC 5424 severity (mapped from
                   clog_level_t: TRACE=7, DEBUG=7, INFO=6, WARN=4,
                   ERROR=3, ALERT=1, FATAL=0)
      TIMESTAMP  = ISO 8601 UTC (same format as logfmt/JSON)
      HOSTNAME   = the result of gethostname(2).  The library keeps a
                   byte only when it is RFC 5424 PRINTUSASCII
                   (0x21-0x7e).  It drops every other byte, and that
                   covers a space and a control character.  It does
                   not truncate the value at that point.  It writes
                   "-" when this leaves no byte at all, and when
                   gethostname(2) itself fails.
      APP-NAME   = the name of the executable, which is the basename
                   of argv[0].  The library filters it to
                   PRINTUSASCII in the same way.  It writes "-" when
                   this leaves no byte at all.
      PID        = getpid(2)
      MSGID      = level string ("INFO", "ERROR", etc.)
      [ccol ...]  = RFC 5424 SD-ELEMENT with SD-ID "ccol" carrying
                   proc="<name>(<pid>):<tname>(<tid>)",
                   src="<file>:<line>", func="<fn>", and any fields
                   that clog_set_field() attached
      MSG        = the formatted message text

    The library escapes a control character with a backslash, as
    \en, \er, \et or \exNN.  That covers a literal newline and a
    carriage return.  It writes a backslash itself as \e\e, so the
    escaped text reads back unambiguously: a real newline appears as
    \en, and the two characters '\e' and 'n' appear as \e\en.  It
    does this in MSG, and in every value of proc, src, func and a
    field.  logfmt uses the same scheme for its own values.  A value
    of proc, src, func or a field is an SD-PARAM-VALUE, which RFC 5424
    requires to be UTF-8.  The library passes a well-formed UTF-8
    sequence through unchanged, and replaces each byte that is not
    part of one with U+FFFD, as the JSON format does.  MSG carries no
    byte order mark, so it is the MSG-ANY form of RFC 5424: a
    receiver must not assume that its bytes are UTF-8, and the
    library passes them through apart from the escapes above.  HOSTNAME and
    APP-NAME can hold no byte that needs
    this escape, because the filter above takes both down to
    PRINTUSASCII.  Together this guarantees one thing.  The content of
    a record can never split that record across several lines.

    clog_set_facility() controls the facility.  It defaults to
    CLOG_SYSLOG_USER.  The library writes a backtrace as separate
    syslog messages, one for each frame.  ccol_log_error,
    ccol_log_alert and ccol_log_fatal produce one.  Each message
    carries the same PRI, TIMESTAMP and MSGID as the original record.
    The library escapes the text of a frame with a backslash, in the
    same way as MSG.  Every frame shares the TIMESTAMP of the original
    record, and does not carry its own write time.  That is what lets
    a reader match a record with its own backtrace frames.

    Connect the fd to a syslog daemon before you call
    clog_set_format().  On Linux that is usually a SOCK_DGRAM Unix
    socket at /dev/log.  Keep each message under 2 KiB, to stay inside
    the usual datagram limits of syslogd.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
logger  Logger handle.
fmt     CLOG_FMT_LOGFMT, CLOG_FMT_JSON, or CLOG_FMT_SYSLOG.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Thread-safe.  The mutex of the shared backing store protects it.
CLOG_FMT_SYSLOG on a logger that sits on a file does nothing, and it
reports nothing.
Asserts when logger is CLOG_INVALID, stale or already closed.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR clog_get_format (3),
.BR clog_set_facility (3),
.BR clog_open_fd_mp (3),
.BR clog_open_file_mp (3),
.BR clog_derive (3),
.BR ccol_set_thread_name (3)
